<script module>
  import Demo from "$ui/components/Demo.abide"
  import ScopedStyleDemo from "$ui/demos/templating/styling/ScopedStyleDemo.abide"
  import NestedStyleDemo from "$ui/demos/templating/styling/NestedStyleDemo.abide"
</script>

<main class="page">
  <h1>Scoped <code>&lt;style&gt;</code></h1>
  <p class="lead">A component-scoped <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> is rewritten at build time: each selector gains a per-component attribute (<code>.swatch</code> → <code>.swatch[data-ab-*]</code>) and abide stamps that attribute on every element the component renders. The styles apply to <strong>this</strong> component only — on the server-rendered HTML and after hydration, never leaking to the rest of the page. (Tailwind classes still work alongside it.)</p>
  <Demo id="scoped" title="Component-scoped <code>&lt;style&gt;</code>" client="src/ui/demos/templating/styling/ScopedStyleDemo.abide">
    <ScopedStyleDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p>Two components render the same <code>.swatch</code> class; only the one that declared the rule is styled by it. The attribute doing that is stamped during SSR, so the scoping is already correct in the initial HTML — hydration changes nothing.</p>
  </aside>
  <Demo id="nested" title="Nested <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> — a subtree scope" client="src/ui/demos/templating/styling/NestedStyleDemo.abide">
    <NestedStyleDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p>A <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> written inside a block scopes that <em>subtree</em>. An element carries every scope in force, so the component's own rule reaches into the branch and the branch's rule reaches nothing outside it — one direction, by construction.</p>
  </aside>
</main>
